```c
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
int subtract(int a, int b) { return a - b; }
int multiply(int a, int b) { return a * b; }

int (*operations[3])(int, int) = {
    &add,
    &subtract,
    &multiply
};
// Example usage: int result = operations[0](10, 5); // result = 15
```
Arrays of function pointers are useful for implementing dispatch tables, state machines, or command pattern.

Using `typedef` for readability:
```c
typedef int (*MathFunc)(int, int);
MathFunc get_operation(char op);
```
Without `typedef` (reading right-to-left):
```c
int (*get_operation(char op))(int, int);
```
This declares `get_operation` as a function taking a `char` and returning a pointer (`*`) to a function that takes `(int, int)` and returns `int`.
